<% content_for :title, "Remindly FAQ — free spoken reminders for a parent who lives alone" %>
<% content_for :description, "Common questions about reminding an older parent to take medication when you do not live with them: what they need, what happens if they miss a dose, what it costs, and what Remindly deliberately does not do." %>

<%#
  Questions and answers live in one place and are rendered twice — as the page a
  person reads, and as the FAQPage graph search engines read. Google requires the
  two to match; keeping a second copy in a JSON literal is how they stop
  matching the first time someone edits a sentence.

  Answers are plain strings, not HTML: FAQPage accepts limited markup and a link
  in an answer buys nothing here, while the strings stay usable as-is in JSON.

  An optional third element is the heading's id, for questions another page
  links to directly. It goes nowhere near the structured data.
%>
<% faqs = [
     [
       "How do I remind an elderly parent to take their medication when I don't live with them?",
       "You set the reminder up from your own phone or computer, and at the time it is due Remindly rings their own telephone and says it out loud — or, if there is a tablet in the house, speaks it from the screen. They do not have to open anything or read anything; on the telephone they press 1 to hear the reminder and 1 again to say it is done; on a screen it is one Done button. For medication, Remindly emails you when it is marked done, or when the time passes and it has not been. That is the part that replaces the phone call: you find out without having to ring and ask."
     ],
     [
       "What does my parent need at their end?",
       "Just their own telephone — a landline or a mobile, in the US or Canada. Remindly rings it when a reminder is due; they press 1 to hear it, then 1 again when it is done. No email address, no password, no account and nothing to install. If there is a tablet or computer in the house, Remindly can speak from that instead: you bookmark a link on it, or read them six numbers down the telephone, and it works best on a device that already sits somewhere they spend time, with the page left open the way a clock sits on a shelf."
     ],
     [
       "What if my parent isn't good with technology?",
       "They do not have to be. There is nothing for them to sign up to and no password to remember, because you create the account and they never need one. On the telephone, their whole part is answering and pressing a key: 1 to hear the reminder, then 1 when it is done, and the first call only asks them to press 1 to agree. If they use a tablet instead, their whole interface is the day's reminders in large type with two buttons, Done and Snooze; the page is bookmarked once and stays working, and the one thing it asks of them is a yes or no, once, on the first screen they see."
     ],
     [
       "Does the page have to stay open?",
       "Only if they use Remindly on a tablet or computer rather than by telephone — and there, yes, it does; this is the honest limitation. Remindly speaks through the browser page, so if the tab is closed nothing is announced. Think of it as leaving a clock on the counter rather than an app you open when you need it. On an iPad there is one extra step: browsers will not play sound until someone has touched the screen, so Remindly asks for a single tap after it starts. Anything it could not say while waiting is spoken once you tap."
     ],
     [
       "How do I know whether they actually took it?",
       "You know whether it was marked done — not whether the tablet was swallowed. At their end they mark a reminder done by pressing 1 on the call or Done on the screen; one nobody answers shows as missed, and you see the whole day at a glance from your own dashboard. For medication, Remindly emails you when a dose is marked done and again if one is missed. A Done is an acknowledgement, not proof: Remindly is not a medical device and not a substitute for care. You choose which kinds of reminder email you — medication is on by default, while hydration and routine reminders happen many times a day and stay off unless you turn them on."
     ],
     [
       "What happens if they don't answer the call?",
       "Remindly tries again — up to three times, five minutes apart, and only inside the calling hours. A voicemail cannot press a key, so an answering machine never counts as an answer and the reminder is never left on one. If nobody answers any attempt, the reminder shows as missed, and you are emailed about it if you get emails for that kind of reminder — medication is on by default, and you can turn it off. A reminder you have marked as one where being late matters is different: every caregiver linked to your parent is emailed, whatever their settings, as soon as the first call goes unanswered — once, not once per attempt — so you hear about it early enough to do something rather than at the end of the day.",
       "if-they-dont-answer"
     ],
     [
       "Can Remindly phone me if my parent doesn't answer?",
       "Yes, for reminders where being late matters. If your parent does not answer any of the calls, Remindly phones the family one at a time, in the order you choose on your parent's page, up to three people. Pressing 1 on that call means you will check on them: the calls stop, and everyone else is emailed to say who is on it. Pressing 2 passes it to the next person straight away; if nobody answers, the next person is rung two minutes later. Each person first adds their own number on their profile and confirms it on a short call, and pressing 9 on any of these calls stops them for you. The call names your parent and the time the reminder was due, never what the reminder was, in case it reaches your voicemail.",
       "if-they-still-dont-answer"
     ],
     [
       "Will my parent think it's a scam call?",
       "That is the worry the call is built around. An automated voice telling an older person to press a key is exactly the shape of the calls this age group is warned about, so every call names Remindly in its first sentence, before it asks for anything, and it never asks for personal details. Being recognisable in the first few seconds matters because a mobile network can strip the caller ID. Nobody is rung with reminders until they have agreed: the first call says who set it up and only asks, and pressing 9 on any call stops the reminder calls."
     ],
     [
       "Can more than one person in the family help?",
       "Yes. Several caregivers can be linked to the same person, each with their own sign-in and their own notification settings, so a sibling who wants every medication alert and one who wants none can both be connected. There are also shared tasks — a specific thing to be done once, which you can assign to a particular caregiver or leave open for whoever gets to it."
     ],
     [
       "How does a caregiver get connected to the person they care for?",
       "Two ways. If your parent has never used Remindly, you set them up yourself — their name and their timezone, and nothing else. If they only have a telephone, add their number and Remindly rings once to ask them, saying who set it up; they press 1 to agree. If there is a tablet or computer, open the link on it and bookmark it, or read them six numbers down the telephone to type in at remindly.care/start. No reminder is spoken and you see no activity until they agree — by pressing 1 on that first call, or on their own screen, which names you when it asks. Saying no — pressing 9 on that first call, or no on the screen — deletes everything you set up; pressing 9 on a later call only stops the calls. The second way is the original one and still works when the person already has an account: they generate a one-time pairing code and share it, and the caregiver enters it. Either side can end the connection at any time, and access stops immediately — nobody is ever locked into an arrangement they did not choose.",
       "connecting"
     ],
     [
       "Is Remindly free?",
       "Yes, while it is in beta. Every reminder, every caregiver you link and every email it sends are free to use. There is no card to enter and no trial that quietly ends. It is early software, and being useful to people matters more right now than being paid for. If charging ever becomes necessary, anyone already using Remindly will be told first, well before anything changes."
     ],
     [
       "Why is Remindly free — what's the catch?",
       "There is no catch, and the honest answer is that Remindly is early software. Having families actually use it, and tell us what is wrong with it, is worth more at this stage than charging for it would be. Your information is not sold and is not used for advertising — the privacy policy sets out exactly what is collected and who sees it. There is no free tier designed to start costing money at the least convenient moment, and no card on file to forget about. If charging ever becomes necessary to keep Remindly running, anyone already using it will hear about it first, and well before anything changes."
     ],
     [
       "Is Remindly a medical device?",
       "No, and it should not be relied on as one. It is a reminder that speaks at a set time and records whether someone marked it done. It does not dispense anything, it cannot tell whether a tablet was actually swallowed, and it is not a substitute for a pill dispenser, a carer, or medical advice. If a missed dose would be dangerous, it should not be the only thing standing between your parent and that dose."
     ],
     [
       "What happens to our data?",
       "Reminders, acknowledgements and the email addresses used to sign in are what Remindly stores. The public pages set no analytics cookies at all. Data is deleted on request — the privacy policy sets out what is kept and for how long."
     ]
   ] %>

<% content_for :structured_data, json_ld(
     "@context" => "https://schema.org",
     "@type" => "FAQPage",
     "mainEntity" => faqs.map { |question, answer, _anchor|
       {
         "@type" => "Question",
         "name" => question,
         "acceptedAnswer" => { "@type" => "Answer", "text" => answer }
       }
     }
   ) %>

<h1>Questions about reminding a parent who lives alone</h1>

<p class="lead">
  Most people find Remindly while working out how to help a parent who is
  managing on their own but forgetting things — a tablet at lunchtime, an
  appointment on Thursday. These are the questions that come up, answered
  plainly, including the ones where the answer is no.
</p>

<% faqs.each do |question, answer, anchor| %>
  <%= tag.h2 question, id: anchor %>
  <p><%= answer %></p>
<% end %>

<h2>Still deciding?</h2>

<p>
  The <a href="<%= how_to_path %>">guide</a> walks through what setting this up
  actually looks like, with short videos. Or email
  <a href="mailto:hello@remindly.care">hello@remindly.care</a> and describe your
  situation — a real person reads it, and if Remindly is the wrong fit for what
  you need, you will be told so.
</p>

<div class="actions">
  <% if current_user %>
    <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="<%= dashboard_path %>">Back to your dashboard</a>
  <% else %>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="<%= sign_in_path %>">Get started — it's free</a>
  <% end %>
</div>
